    ]
   },
   "section": "hook",
   "overlays": {
    "hook": "She ran away from George Washington. *He never stopped hunting her.*"
   },
   "captions": false,
   "speed": 1.4
  },
  {
   "id": "name",
   "text": "Her name was Ona Judge.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "Ona Judge",
    "sub": "about 1773 to 1848"
   },
   "section": "who"
  },
  {
   "id": "born",
   "text": "She was born enslaved at Mount Vernon, and became Martha Washington's personal maid.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "photo",
    "image": "mw",
    "focus": [
     0.47,
     0.33
    ],
    "zoom": [
     2.0,
     2.15
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.3
    ]
   },
   "section": "story"
  },
  {
   "id": "gift",
   "text": "In 1796, she learned she would be given away, to Martha's granddaughter.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "eliza",
    "caption": "Eliza Custis Law, Martha's granddaughter"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "dinner",
   "text": "So on May 21, 1796, while the President ate dinner, she walked out the door.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "house",
    "caption": "The President's House, Philadelphia"
   },
   "overlays": {
    "stamp": "21 May 1796"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "ship",
   "text": "Free Black friends got her onto a ship, bound for Portsmouth, New Hampshire.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "ferry",
    "caption": "The Philadelphia waterfront, 1800"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "ad",
   "text": "A newspaper ad offered ten dollars to bring her back.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "ad",
    "caption": "Runaway ad for Oney Judge, May 1796"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "found",
   "text": "Washington found her. He sent a customs officer to put her on a ship back. She refused.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "photo",
    "image": "gw",
    "focus": [
     0.52,
     0.3
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.5,
     1.65
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.35
    ]
   },
   "section": "twist"
  },
  {
   "id": "offer",
   "text": "She offered to return, if they promised to free her someday. Washington said no.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "Totally inadmissible",
    "sub": "Washington, 1796"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "nephew",
   "text": "In 1799, he sent his nephew to seize her. A senator secretly warned her, and she hid.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "1799",
    "sub": "a second attempt"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "died",
   "text": "That December, Washington died. She was never caught.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "family",
    "caption": "The Washington Family, by Edward Savage"
   },
   "section": "legacy"
  },
  {
   "id": "free",
   "text": "Asked if she regretted running, she said: No, I am free.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "\"No, I am free.\"",
    "sub": "Ona Judge, 1845"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "end",
   "text": "She died in 1848. By law, she was still a fugitive.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "photo",
    "image": "house",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.5
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.05,
     1.12
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.5
    ]
   }
  },
